title: "Amortized R(t): a time-varying-beta SIR model across all US states"
description: >
  Extend the stochastic SIR case study to a contact rate that changes over
  time, choose how many spline knots the data can support, and train one
  amortized posterior that infers the effective reproduction number R(t) for
  every US state from the first 120 days of the SARS-CoV-2 pandemic.
output: rmarkdown::html_vignette
vignette: >
  %\VignetteIndexEntry{Amortized R(t): a time-varying-beta SIR model across all US states}
  %\VignetteEngine{knitr::rmarkdown_notangle}
  %\VignetteEncoding{UTF-8}
---

```{r, include = FALSE}
# This vignette is precomputed: see vignettes/precompute.R. Edit the .Rmd.orig,
# re-run the precompute script, and commit the regenerated .Rmd and figures.
# Regenerating it needs internet access (to pull case counts and population
# data) as well as torch.
knitr::opts_chunk$set(
  collapse = TRUE, comment = "#>", error = FALSE,
  fig.path = "figures/sir-rt-", fig.width = 6.5, fig.height = 4.5,
  dpi = 96, fig.retina = NULL
)
set.seed(1)
```

`vignette("sir-epidemic")` fits a stochastic SIR outbreak with a *constant*
contact rate \(\beta\). Real epidemics do not hold still: control measures,
behavior change, and depleting susceptibles all move the transmission rate
over the course of an outbreak. This vignette lets \(\beta\) vary with time,
\(\beta(t)\), and asks the question public-health teams actually care about:
how did the **effective reproduction number**
\(R_e(t) = \beta(t) S(t) / (N \gamma)\) evolve? We answer it for all 50 US
states plus Washington, DC, over the first 120 days of the SARS-CoV-2
pandemic (2020-01-21 to 2020-05-19), training **one** amortized posterior and
conditioning it on each state's reported-case curve in turn.

Two modeling questions have to be answered before any of that: how many
points does \(\beta(t)\) need, and where do they go? We answer both from the
data rather than by assumption.

## The data: reported cases in the first 120 days

We use the New York Times' county/state-level COVID-19 case counts, which
start on 2020-01-21 — the date of the first confirmed US case (Snohomish
County, Washington) — and US Census population estimates to convert case
counts to a proportion of each state's population.

```{r}
cases_url <- "https://raw.githubusercontent.com/nytimes/covid-19-data/master/us-states.csv"
pop_url <- "https://raw.githubusercontent.com/COVID19Tracking/associated-data/master/us_census_data/us_census_2018_population_estimates_states.csv"

raw_cases <- read.csv(cases_url)
pop_table <- read.csv(pop_url)

raw_cases$date <- as.Date(raw_cases$date)
start_date <- as.Date("2020-01-21")
end_date <- start_date + 119L   # 120 days, day 1 .. day 120
window <- raw_cases[raw_cases$date >= start_date & raw_cases$date <= end_date, ]

# the 50 states + DC only -- drop territories (Puerto Rico is the only one the
# population table also carries, so it needs an explicit exclusion)
state_names <- sort(setdiff(intersect(unique(window$state), pop_table$state_name),
                            "Puerto Rico"))
window <- window[window$state %in% state_names, ]
length(state_names)
```

Daily counts are noisy (weekend reporting lulls, occasional data-revision
corrections that make the cumulative count dip). We convert each state's
cumulative series to new cases per day, clip negative revisions to zero, and
aggregate into 15 non-overlapping 8-day bins — coarse enough to smooth
weekday effects, still fine enough to trace the rise and fall of the first
wave.

```{r}
n_bins <- 15L
bin_len <- 8L   # 15 * 8 = 120 days
days <- n_bins * bin_len

new_cases_binned <- function(state_df) {
  full <- data.frame(date = seq(start_date, end_date, by = "day"))
  full <- merge(full, state_df[, c("date", "cases")], by = "date", all.x = TRUE)
  full$cases[is.na(full$cases)] <- 0
  full$cases <- cummax(full$cases)             # enforce a monotone cumulative count
  new <- c(full$cases[1], diff(full$cases))
  new[new < 0] <- 0                             # data-revision artifacts
  bin <- rep(seq_len(n_bins), each = bin_len)
  as.numeric(tapply(new, bin, sum))
}

case_bins <- t(sapply(state_names, function(s) new_cases_binned(window[window$state == s, ])))
rownames(case_bins) <- state_names
pop <- stats::setNames(pop_table$population, pop_table$state_name)[state_names]
range(pop)
```

State populations span two orders of magnitude, from Wyoming
(`r format(pop["Wyoming"], big.mark = ",")`) up to California
(`r format(max(pop), big.mark = ",")`). That range matters for the simulator
below: population size is a *known* covariate, not something we infer, but
the amortized posterior still has to condition on it correctly for a
39-million-person state and a 600,000-person state alike.

The national aggregate is the curve we will use to choose \(\beta(t)\)'s
knots.

```{r national-curve, fig.alt = "National new reported cases per day, 7-day rolling average, first 120 days."}
national <- aggregate(cases ~ date, data = window, sum)
national <- national[order(national$date), ]
national$new <- pmax(c(national$cases[1], diff(national$cases)), 0)
roll7 <- function(x, k = 7) sapply(seq_along(x), function(i) mean(x[max(1, i - k + 1):i]))
national$new7 <- roll7(national$new)
national$day <- as.numeric(national$date - start_date) + 1

plot(national$date, national$new7, type = "l", lwd = 2,
     xlab = "date", ylab = "new cases/day (7-day average)",
     main = "US reported cases, first 120 days")
```

## How many knots, and where?

We model \(\log \beta(t)\) as **piecewise linear between a small number of
knots** — the simplest spline that can bend. Too few knots and \(\beta(t)\)
cannot represent the epidemic's actual shape (seeding, acceleration, control);
too many and the amortized posterior is trying to pin down parameters the
data cannot separately identify, which is exactly the overfitting SBC would
later expose as miscalibration.

We pick the number and placement by fitting a continuous piecewise-linear
(linear-spline) regression to the log of the national curve, `log(new7)`, and
selecting breakpoints by BIC — the standard bias/variance trade-off for
segmented regression. For each candidate breakpoint count \(k\), we grid-search
breakpoint locations (5-day grid) for the placement that minimizes the
residual sum of squares, and track how much BIC improves from \(k\) to
\(k+1\).

```{r knot-selection}
nat <- national[national$new7 > 0, ]
y <- log(nat$new7)
day <- nat$day
n_obs <- length(y)

fit_bic <- function(taus) {
  X <- cbind(1, day, sapply(taus, function(tau) pmax(day - tau, 0)))
  fit <- lm.fit(X, y)
  if (any(is.na(fit$coefficients))) return(NULL)
  rss <- sum(fit$residuals^2)
  p <- ncol(X)
  list(bic = n_obs * log(rss / n_obs) + p * log(n_obs), taus = taus)
}

cand <- seq(10, 110, by = 5)
best_by_k <- lapply(0:6, function(k) {
  if (k == 0) return(fit_bic(numeric(0)))
  combs <- combn(cand, k)
  best <- NULL
  for (j in seq_len(ncol(combs))) {
    r <- fit_bic(combs[, j])
    if (!is.null(r) && (is.null(best) || r$bic < best$bic)) best <- r
  }
  best
})

knot_table <- data.frame(
  breakpoints = 0:6,
  bic = sapply(best_by_k, `[[`, "bic"),
  taus = sapply(best_by_k, function(b) paste(b$taus, collapse = ", "))
)
knot_table$delta_bic <- c(NA, -diff(knot_table$bic))
knot_table
```

BIC keeps improving out to 6 breakpoints, so raw BIC alone does not pick a
clear winner — a second signal does. Compare the breakpoint sets themselves:
going from 2 to 3 breakpoints *refines* the previous solution ({30, 70}
becomes {25, 40, 70}, i.e. the existing breakpoints move slightly and one is
added), but going from 3 to 4 does not — {25, 40, 70} is replaced wholesale by
{15, 20, 40, 70}, dropping day 25 and inserting two breakpoints only 5 days
apart, both inside the window where national counts were in the single or
low double digits. That is the signature of a search fitting noise in small
counts rather than finding new structure: real inflections give stable
refinements as `k` grows, spurious ones cause the whole configuration to
jump. Beyond 4 breakpoints the configurations keep reshuffling in the same
way, and the marginal BIC gain (`delta_bic`) has by then dropped an order of
magnitude from its peak. We take **3 breakpoints** — 4 constant segments in
\(\log \beta(t)\) — as the last stable, well-supported fit. Combined with the
two endpoints (day 1 and day 120), that gives **5 boundary days**:

```{r}
knot_days <- c(1, sort(best_by_k[[4]]$taus), 120)
knot_days
```

Those knots line up with the actual course of the US epidemic: day 1
(2020-01-21, the first detected case) through the low, mostly-imported count
of late January and February; an inflection around day
`r knot_days[2]` (`r format(start_date + knot_days[2] - 1, "%b %d")`) as
community transmission took hold; a second inflection near day
`r knot_days[3]` (`r format(start_date + knot_days[3] - 1, "%b %d")`), close
to the WHO's March 11 pandemic declaration and the first stay-at-home orders;
a third near day `r knot_days[4]` (`r format(start_date + knot_days[4] - 1, "%b %d")`),
by which point most states had issued stay-at-home orders and growth was
decelerating; and day 120 (2020-05-19), by which point the first wave had
largely plateaued nationally. We are not fitting these dates to epidemiology
by hand — they fell out of the BIC search — but they recover a shape anyone
who lived through spring 2020 will recognize.

```{r knot-plot, fig.alt = "National curve on the log scale with the selected knot days marked."}
plot(national$date, log1p(national$new7), type = "l", lwd = 2,
     xlab = "date", ylab = "log(1 + new cases/day)")
abline(v = start_date + knot_days - 1, col = "firebrick", lty = 2, lwd = 2)
```

## The model: a stochastic SIR with a piecewise-constant \(\log \beta(t)\)

The dynamics are the same discrete-time, binomial-transition SIR used in `vignette("sir-epidemic")` — susceptible, infected, and recovered counts update via `rbinom()`, so the process is genuinely stochastic and there is no tractable likelihood for the reported-case curve. Susceptibles deplete: every infection removes someone from \(S\), and that feedback is what separates the *effective* reproduction number from the contact rate. Three things are new relative to the constant-\(\beta\) vignette.

**\(\beta\) is piecewise constant in time.** Between each pair of adjacent breakpoints \(\beta(t)\) is held at a free parameter \(\beta_k\) — a flat contact rate for the duration of a regime. At the 3 interior breakpoints the level either side blends into the other over a smooth, roughly one-week **logistic** transition rather than switching in a single day: behavior change and policy roll-out (school closures, stay-at-home orders) unfold over days, not instantaneously.

The breakpoint days get **wiggle room per state**: the national BIC search picks a shared, nominal set of breakpoints, but community transmission did not take hold, and stay-at-home orders did not land, on the same calendar day everywhere. Rather than move each breakpoint independently — which risks two adjacent breakpoints crossing — we put a prior on each **regime's duration** \(d_k\) and take the breakpoints as cumulative sums, so the regimes tile the window in order for every draw. Only 3 of the 4 durations are free: the simulator rescales the durations to span exactly the 119 days between day 1 and day 120, so their common scale does nothing. Parameterizing the free directions directly (three log-ratios against the last regime, which acts as the reference) keeps every coordinate of \(\theta\) identified. A fourth coordinate would be pure noise for the flow to model.

**The epidemic starts when it starts.** The single most consequential change from a naive version of this model: the day the virus arrives, \(t_0\), and the number of infections \(I_0\) that arrive with it are **inferred**, not fixed. Seeding every state with a couple of infections on 2020-01-21 gets the epidemiology wrong twice over. It is wrong about the world — Washington's outbreak was seeded in January, West Virginia did not report a case until March 17 — and it is wrong about what a two-infection seed does to a 120-day simulation. A supercritical branching process started from two individuals has a substantial extinction probability and, conditional on survival, a final size spread over orders of magnitude; the *timing* of takeoff then wanders by weeks. Run that forward and the posterior predictive is a smear whose mean sits far above any actual epidemic. Inferring \((t_0, I_0)\) pins the phase with a parameter rather than leaving it to luck, and it is the more faithful model besides: states were seeded by many independent importations, not by a single index case.

**Ascertainment is pinned by seroprevalence, not left free.** Only a fraction \(\rho\) of infections became a confirmed case in spring 2020. \(\rho\) is inferred, but with an *informative* prior, and that is not optional. Reported case counts identify the product \(\rho \times \text{incidence}\), not either factor: a small epidemic that is well ascertained and an enormous one that is barely ascertained produce the same curve. Left to itself, the fit takes the second branch — it buys a broad, flat-topped epidemic peak cheaply by burning through the susceptible pool, which requires \(\rho\) in the low thousandths and an attack rate near 1. Serological surveys settle it from outside the case data: CDC's large-scale commercial-lab study put spring-2020 infections at roughly 6 to 24 times reported cases across 10 US sites (Havers et al. 2020, *JAMA Internal Medicine*), i.e. \(\rho\) between about 0.04 and 0.17. We encode that as a logit-normal prior centered at 0.10 with 95% mass in \([0.05, 0.19]\).

Finally, **population size \(N\)** is a *known* covariate, not a free parameter: the simulator draws it from a distribution spanning the range of actual state populations, and the density estimator conditions on it directly (as \(\log N\), passed alongside the case-count data) so that one trained network generalizes from Wyoming to California. The recovery rate \(\gamma\) is **fixed**: with only case counts (no recovery or serology time series) the generation interval is not separately identifiable from \(\beta(t)\), so we set \(1/\gamma = 7\) days, in line with early estimates of the COVID-19 serial interval (Li et al. 2020, *NEJM*).

```{r}
library(neuralsbi)

n_knots <- length(knot_days)
n_segments <- n_knots - 1L               # 4 constant-beta regimes
nominal_dur <- diff(knot_days)           # nominal (national) duration of each regime
total_days <- sum(nominal_dur)           # 119: span covered day 1 -> day 120
transition_days <- 7                     # ~1-week logistic transition per breakpoint
transition_scale <- transition_days / 4
gamma_fixed <- 1 / 7
t_grid <- seq_len(days)
bin_idx <- rep(seq_len(n_bins), each = bin_len)

param_names <- c(paste0("beta[", seq_len(n_segments), "]"),
                 paste0("d[", seq_len(n_segments - 1L), "]"),
                 "t[0]", "I[0]", "rho")
prior <- prior_normal(
  mean = stats::setNames(
    c(rep(log(0.28), n_segments),                                   # R = beta/gamma ~ 2
      log(nominal_dur[-n_segments] / nominal_dur[n_segments]),      # regime durations
      qlogis(35 / days),                                            # introduction day
      log(30),                                                      # infections seeded
      qlogis(0.10)),                                                # ascertainment
    param_names),
  sd = c(rep(0.6, n_segments), rep(0.35, n_segments - 1L), 1.1, 1.2, 0.4)
)
```

\(\theta\) is 10-dimensional and unconstrained on \(\mathbb{R}^{10}\) — the log/logit transforms mean a single Gaussian prior with no truncated support, so the posterior needs no rejection sampling for leakage. Naming `prior`'s `mean` vector carries those names through every posterior draw, SBC result, and diagnostic plot below.

The prior on each \(\log \beta_k\) is centered on \(R = \beta/\gamma = 2\) with 95% mass between 0.6 and 6.4. That is deliberately wide in both directions: a regime may be an unmitigated wild-type epidemic (\(R \approx 3\)) or a suppressed one (\(R \approx 0.8\)), and which is which is exactly what we are asking the data. The introduction day has 95% prior mass between roughly 2020-01-26 and 2020-04-20, and the seed size between about 3 and 300 infections.

```{r}
sir_rt_simulator <- function(theta, N_fixed = NULL, full = FALSE) {
  theta <- matrix(as.numeric(theta), ncol = 2L * n_segments + 2L)
  n <- nrow(theta)
  log_beta_segments <- theta[, seq_len(n_segments), drop = FALSE]
  log_dur_ratio <- theta[, n_segments + seq_len(n_segments - 1L), drop = FALSE]
  t0 <- 1 + (days - 1) * plogis(theta[, 2L * n_segments])
  seed_mean <- exp(theta[, 2L * n_segments + 1L])
  rho <- plogis(theta[, 2L * n_segments + 2L])

  # population: a known covariate, drawn to span real US state sizes during
  # training and fixed to the observed state's population at inference time
  N <- if (is.null(N_fixed)) exp(stats::runif(n, log(5e5), log(4e7))) else rep(N_fixed, n)

  # regime durations from 3 free log-ratios (the last regime is the reference),
  # rescaled so the 4 regimes tile the 119-day window exactly. Their cumulative
  # sum is increasing by construction, so breakpoints cannot collide or cross.
  w <- cbind(exp(log_dur_ratio), 1)
  dur <- w / rowSums(w) * total_days
  tau <- knot_days[1] + t(apply(dur, 1, cumsum))[, seq_len(n_segments - 1L), drop = FALSE]

  w_ext <- c(list(matrix(1, n, days)),
             lapply(seq_len(n_segments - 1L), function(k) {
               outer(tau[, k], t_grid, function(a, t) plogis((t - a) / transition_scale))
             }),
             list(matrix(0, n, days)))
  beta_t <- exp(Reduce(`+`, lapply(seq_len(n_segments), function(j) {
    (w_ext[[j]] - w_ext[[j + 1]]) * log_beta_segments[, j]
  })))

  S <- N; I <- rep(0, n); seeded <- rep(FALSE, n)
  incidence <- matrix(0, n, days); S_frac <- matrix(0, n, days)
  n_sub <- 2L; dt <- 1 / n_sub
  p_rec <- 1 - exp(-gamma_fixed * dt)
  for (t in t_grid) {
    fresh <- !seeded & (t >= t0)                 # the state's introduction
    if (any(fresh)) {
      s0 <- pmin(stats::rpois(sum(fresh), seed_mean[fresh]), S[fresh])
      I[fresh] <- I[fresh] + s0
      S[fresh] <- S[fresh] - s0
      seeded[fresh] <- TRUE
    }
    H <- rep(0, n); beta <- beta_t[, t]
    for (s in seq_len(n_sub)) {
      p_inf <- pmin(pmax(1 - exp(-beta * I / N * dt), 0), 1)
      dN_SI <- stats::rbinom(n, pmax(round(S), 0), p_inf)
      dN_IR <- stats::rbinom(n, pmax(round(I), 0), p_rec)
      S <- S - dN_SI; I <- I + dN_SI - dN_IR    # susceptibles deplete
      H <- H + dN_SI
    }
    incidence[, t] <- H
    S_frac[, t] <- S / N
  }
  reported <- matrix(stats::rbinom(n * days, pmax(round(incidence), 0), rho), n, days)
  x_bins <- t(apply(reported, 1, function(row) tapply(row, bin_idx, sum)))
  x <- cbind(log(N), log1p(x_bins))   # log(N) + 15 binned, log1p-compressed counts
  if (!full) return(x)
  # `full = TRUE` also returns the latent paths, so R(t) below can use the same
  # simulator rather than a re-implementation of beta(t) that could drift from it
  list(x = x, beta_t = beta_t, S_frac = S_frac, incidence = incidence)
}
```

## Check the prior predictive before training anything

Amortized NPE learns \(p(\theta \mid x)\) over the *prior predictive* distribution of \(x\). Data that the prior predictive essentially never produces sit in a region where the trained network is extrapolating, and its posterior there is not to be trusted — no amount of training fixes it. So before spending a simulation budget, check that the model can produce curves like the ones we intend to condition on.

```{r prior-predictive}
bin_mid <- (seq_len(n_bins) - 1) * bin_len + (bin_len + 1) / 2
bin_date <- start_date + bin_mid - 1

set.seed(11)
n_pp <- 2000
theta_pp <- sample_prior(prior, n_pp)

pp_for <- function(state) {
  x <- sir_rt_simulator(theta_pp, N_fixed = pop[[state]])
  expm1(x[, -1, drop = FALSE])
}

check_state <- function(state) {
  cnt <- pp_for(state)
  d <- rowMeans(abs(log1p(cnt) - rep(log1p(case_bins[state, ]), each = n_pp)))
  c(within_e = mean(d < 1), best = min(d))
}
round(t(sapply(c("Michigan", "West Virginia", "New York"), check_state)), 3)
```

A few percent of prior draws land within a factor of \(e\) of each observed curve, averaged over the 15 bins. That is the number to watch: it is small — the prior is deliberately vague — but it is not a rounding error, so the observations sit inside the bulk of the prior predictive rather than out in its tail.

```{r prior-pred-plot, fig.width = 7, fig.height = 4, fig.alt = "Prior predictive envelope on a log scale with two observed state curves inside it."}
mi <- pp_for("Michigan")
env <- apply(log1p(mi), 2, stats::quantile, c(0.05, 0.5, 0.95))
plot(bin_date, env[2, ], type = "n", ylim = range(env), xlab = "date (2020)",
     ylab = "log(1 + cases per 8-day bin)",
     main = "Prior predictive, Michigan-sized population")
polygon(c(bin_date, rev(bin_date)), c(env[1, ], rev(env[3, ])),
        col = "grey85", border = NA)
lines(bin_date, env[2, ], lwd = 2, col = "grey40")
lines(bin_date, log1p(case_bins["Michigan", ]), lwd = 2, col = "firebrick")
lines(bin_date, log1p(case_bins["West Virginia", ]), lwd = 2, col = "steelblue")
legend("topleft", bty = "n", lwd = 2, col = c("grey40", "firebrick", "steelblue"),
       legend = c("prior predictive median (90% band)", "Michigan", "West Virginia"))
```

Both observed curves stay inside the 90% prior predictive band for their whole length. Fixing the seeding day at day 1 instead of inferring it fails exactly this check: West Virginia's seven empty bins can then only be produced by a sub-threshold contact rate, which usually means extinction, so the observed curve drops out of the band's lower edge.

## Training the amortized posterior

One MAF is trained across the whole prior — including the full range of state population sizes — and reused for every state below. This is what *amortized* buys: train once, then condition on 51 different observations for the price of a forward pass each.

```{r fit-npe}
fit <- npe(prior, sir_rt_simulator, n_simulations = 20000,
           density_estimator = "maf", max_epochs = 300, n_restarts = 1,
           seed = 1, verbose = FALSE)
fit
```

## Validate before trusting a single R(t) curve

Simulation-based calibration (SBC) checks that the posterior's credible intervals have the coverage they claim, using fresh prior draws the network never saw during training. For each of many simulated "true" parameter values we compute where that true value ranks among posterior draws conditioned on the resulting simulated data; if the posterior is calibrated, that rank is equally likely to fall anywhere, so the histogram of ranks across trials should come out flat. A histogram skewed toward the edges means the posterior is overconfident (true value often falls outside the bulk of the posterior); skewed toward the middle means underconfident. The red dashed line marks the count expected under perfect calibration, and the dotted lines mark a 99% band around it, so any bar that pokes outside the dotted lines is a genuine, not just noisy, departure from flat.

```{r sbc}
sbc_res <- sbc(fit, sir_rt_simulator, n_sbc = 150, n_posterior_samples = 300, seed = 2)
sbc_res    # one uniformity p-value per parameter
```

Four parameters are worth looking at directly: \(\log \beta\) in the final regime (day 120 — the most policy-relevant point, closest to "now" in this window), the introduction day \(t_0\) and seed size \(I_0\) (the new, previously-fixed pieces of the model, and the two most likely to trade off against each other), and the ascertainment rate \(\rho\). Because `prior`'s `mean` was a named vector, `sbc_res` carries those names, and `plot_sbc()` renders them as their plotmath symbol directly in the panel title instead of a generic "parameter 4".

```{r sbc-beta4, fig.alt = "SBC rank histogram for log-beta in the final (day 120) regime; a flat histogram indicates calibration."}
plot_sbc(sbc_res, param = n_segments)
```

```{r sbc-t0, fig.alt = "SBC rank histogram for the introduction day; a flat histogram indicates calibration."}
plot_sbc(sbc_res, param = 2L * n_segments)
```

```{r sbc-seed, fig.alt = "SBC rank histogram for the number of infections seeded at the introduction; a flat histogram indicates calibration."}
plot_sbc(sbc_res, param = 2L * n_segments + 1L)
```

```{r sbc-rho, fig.alt = "SBC rank histogram for the ascertainment rate rho; a flat histogram indicates calibration."}
plot_sbc(sbc_res, param = 2L * n_segments + 2L)
```

And a posterior predictive check against real data grounds the calibration check in the actual observations we care about: does the fitted model, run forward from its posterior, reproduce the shape of a real state's curve? We check six states that span very different trajectories: Washington (site of the first detected US case), New York (the largest epicenter in this window), California (first state to issue a stay-at-home order), Louisiana (a sharp early surge tied to Mardi Gras), Michigan (a severe Midwest outbreak, including major nursing-home clusters), and West Virginia (the last state to report a case).

We summarize the predictive by its **median**, not its mean. Case counts here span four orders of magnitude and the estimator is trained on \(\log(1 + x)\); the predictive distribution in each bin is strongly right-skewed, so its mean tracks the upper tail rather than the center. Plotting the mean of a skewed count predictive makes a well-calibrated fit look like a systematic overshoot.

```{r post-pred-states, fig.width = 7, fig.height = 6, fig.alt = "Posterior predictive check overlaying reported cases and the predictive interval for six representative states."}
highlight <- c("Washington", "New York", "California", "Louisiana", "Michigan", "West Virginia")

post_pred_state <- function(state, n_draws = 500) {
  x_obs <- c(log(pop[[state]]), log1p(case_bins[state, ]))
  post <- posterior(fit, x_obs = x_obs)
  sim_fixed <- function(theta) sir_rt_simulator(theta, N_fixed = pop[[state]])
  pred <- posterior_predictive(post, sim_fixed, n = n_draws)
  pred_counts <- expm1(pred[, -1, drop = FALSE])  # drop log(N); undo log1p
  data.frame(state = state, date = bin_date,
             observed = case_bins[state, ],
             pred_med = apply(pred_counts, 2, stats::median),
             pred_lo = apply(pred_counts, 2, stats::quantile, 0.05),
             pred_hi = apply(pred_counts, 2, stats::quantile, 0.95))
}

post_pred_all <- do.call(rbind, lapply(highlight, post_pred_state))
post_pred_all$state <- factor(post_pred_all$state, levels = highlight)

library(ggplot2)
ggplot(post_pred_all, aes(date)) +
  geom_ribbon(aes(ymin = pred_lo, ymax = pred_hi), fill = "steelblue", alpha = 0.25) +
  geom_line(aes(y = pred_med), colour = "steelblue", linewidth = 0.8) +
  geom_point(aes(y = observed), colour = "black", size = 1.4) +
  facet_wrap(~state, ncol = 3, scales = "free_y") +
  scale_x_date(date_labels = "%b %d") +
  labs(x = "date (2020)", y = "new cases per 8-day bin",
       title = "Posterior predictive check, 6 representative states",
       subtitle = "points: reported cases; line: predictive median; band: 90% interval") +
  theme_minimal() +
  theme(axis.text.x = element_text(angle = 45, hjust = 1))
```

The predictive median tracks each state's peak, including its height, and the observed points stay inside the 90% band across four orders of magnitude of epidemic size — from West Virginia's few hundred cases per bin to New York's tens of thousands.

## R(t) for every state

For each of the 51 jurisdictions we condition the same trained posterior on that state's own case-count curve and population, then push each posterior draw back through the *same simulator* to get its contact rate \(\beta(t)\) and its susceptible fraction \(S(t)/N\). The effective reproduction number is
\[
R_e(t) \;=\; \frac{\beta(t)}{\gamma} \cdot \frac{S(t)}{N},
\]
the average number of secondary cases per infection *given the pool of susceptibles still available*. The \(S(t)/N\) factor is not decoration: in New York the model infers that a substantial share of the population had been infected by mid-May, so \(R_e\) there ends the window well below the contact rate alone would suggest. Computing it from the simulator's own state trajectories, rather than re-deriving \(\beta(t)\) alongside it, keeps the reported quantity and the fitted model from drifting apart.

```{r rt-all-states}
rt_state <- function(state, n_draws = 500) {
  x_obs <- c(log(pop[[state]]), log1p(case_bins[state, ]))
  draws <- sample(posterior(fit, x_obs = x_obs), n_draws)
  sim <- sir_rt_simulator(draws, N_fixed = pop[[state]], full = TRUE)
  rt <- sim$beta_t / gamma_fixed * sim$S_frac
  data.frame(state = state, date = start_date + t_grid - 1,
             rt_med = apply(rt, 2, stats::median),
             rt_lo = apply(rt, 2, stats::quantile, 0.05),
             rt_hi = apply(rt, 2, stats::quantile, 0.95),
             attack = mean(1 - sim$S_frac[, days]))
}

rt_all <- do.call(rbind, lapply(state_names, rt_state))
```

The inferred attack rates are the sanity check on the ascertainment prior: they are what \(\rho\) and the case counts imply about how much of each state had actually been infected by 2020-05-19.

```{r attack-rates}
attack_by_state <- tapply(rt_all$attack, rt_all$state, `[`, 1)
round(quantile(attack_by_state, c(0, 0.25, 0.5, 0.75, 1)), 4)
round(sort(attack_by_state, decreasing = TRUE)[1:5], 3)
```

A handful of states tell the national story: Washington (site of the first detected US case and the Kirkland nursing-home cluster), New York (the largest epicenter in this window), California (first state to issue a stay-at-home order, March 19), Louisiana (a sharp early surge tied to Mardi Gras gatherings in New Orleans), Michigan (a severe Midwest outbreak with its own major nursing-home clusters), and West Virginia (the last state to report a case, on March 17).

```{r rt-highlight, fig.width = 7, fig.height = 5, fig.alt = "Effective reproduction number trajectories with 90 percent credible bands for six representative states."}
rt_highlight <- rt_all[rt_all$state %in% highlight, ]
rt_highlight$state <- factor(rt_highlight$state, levels = highlight)

ggplot(rt_highlight, aes(date, rt_med)) +
  geom_ribbon(aes(ymin = rt_lo, ymax = rt_hi), fill = "steelblue", alpha = 0.25) +
  geom_line(colour = "steelblue", linewidth = 0.8) +
  geom_hline(yintercept = 1, linetype = 2, colour = "grey40") +
  facet_wrap(~state, ncol = 3) +
  scale_x_date(date_labels = "%b %d") +
  labs(x = "date (2020)", y = expression(R[e](t)),
       title = expression(paste("Posterior ", R[e], "(t), 6 representative states")),
       subtitle = "shaded band: 90% credible interval; dashed line: R = 1") +
  theme_minimal() +
  theme(axis.text.x = element_text(angle = 45, hjust = 1))
```

All 51 states, condensed into one view: a heatmap of posterior-median \(R_e(t)\), states sorted by their peak reproduction number.

```{r rt-heatmap, fig.width = 7, fig.height = 8, fig.alt = "Heatmap of posterior median effective reproduction number across all US states over the first 120 days."}
peak_order <- names(sort(tapply(rt_all$rt_med, rt_all$state, max), decreasing = TRUE))
rt_all$state <- factor(rt_all$state, levels = rev(peak_order))

ggplot(rt_all, aes(date, state, fill = rt_med)) +
  geom_raster() +
  scale_fill_gradient2(midpoint = 1, low = "steelblue", mid = "white",
                       high = "firebrick", name = expression(R[e](t))) +
  scale_x_date(date_labels = "%b %d") +
  labs(x = "date (2020)", y = NULL,
       title = expression(paste("Posterior median ", R[e], "(t), all 50 states + DC")),
       subtitle = "sorted by peak; white = R = 1") +
  theme_minimal() +
  theme(axis.text.y = element_text(size = 6))
```

The pattern matches the national narrative from the knot-selection curve: \(R_e(t)\) is flat and uninformative before a state's inferred introduction day (no epidemic, nothing in the data to constrain \(\beta\), so the posterior there is close to the prior), climbs through early-to-mid March as community transmission spreads, and comes back down toward and below 1 by mid-to-late April as stay-at-home orders took effect nearly everywhere — with real heterogeneity in timing and magnitude that a single national estimate would have hidden.

## What amortization bought here

The alternative to amortized NPE is fitting a separate model per state — 51 particle filters, 51 MCMC chains, 51 opportunities for a chain to fail to converge. Here, training happened once (`r fit$n_simulations` simulations, one MAF), and every state's posterior above cost only a forward pass through the trained network. That is the payoff `vignette("sir-epidemic")` promised in the abstract; this vignette spends it on a real, 51-jurisdiction dataset.

Two of the modeling choices above are worth restating as general lessons, because they are the ones that decide whether this fit works at all.

*Do not leave the epidemic's phase to demographic noise.* A branching process started from a couple of infections and run for four months has a final size spread across orders of magnitude and a takeoff time that wanders by weeks. If nothing in \(\theta\) can absorb that, the posterior predictive integrates over it: replicate simulations at a *single, fixed* \(\theta\) then differ by a factor of several thousand at the peak bin. Inferring \((t_0, I_0)\) brings that down to a factor of about three, which is genuine early-epidemic stochasticity rather than an artifact of the seeding convention. The recovered introduction days are a free check on the change — the posterior puts New York's near March 1 and West Virginia's in mid-March, matching when those states actually reported their first cases.

*Do not let case counts alone set the epidemic's scale.* Reported cases identify \(\rho \times \text{incidence}\). Without external information on \(\rho\), the fit is free to slide along that ridge, and it slides toward vast, barely-ascertained epidemics because susceptible depletion is then available to flatten the peak. Seroprevalence data are what break the tie, and they belong in the prior.

The model still carries real simplifications, worth stating plainly. \(\gamma\) is fixed rather than inferred, so `sbc()` above is calibrated conditional on that choice, not unconditionally. Every state shares the same *number* and rough *national* placement of regimes, with only a per-state prior on each regime's duration letting the exact inflection days move; a state whose epidemic unfolded on a genuinely different schedule, rather than just a stretched or compressed one, is not well served by this. The one-week width of the logistic transition between segments is a modeling choice representing how quickly behavior or policy shifted, not something the case counts separately identify. A single ascertainment rate \(\rho\) stands in for what was, in practice, a patchwork of state and local testing capacity that grew steeply over exactly this window — a time-varying \(\rho(t)\) is the most obvious next refinement, at the cost of another parameter confounded with \(\beta(t)\). And the model gives each state one introduction, whereas states with large international airports saw a trickle of imported cases for weeks before community transmission established; California, whose first four bins carry a handful of traveler-associated cases, is the state this fits worst. Loosening any of these is a change to the simulator, not to the inference machinery around it — the same amortized-NPE workflow applies unchanged.

For the underlying workflow in more depth, see `vignette("sir-epidemic")` and `vignette("diagnostics")`.
